      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "---\n",
        "title: \"Lab 2: Modelling (practice)\"\n",
        "author: \"Ashley Walsh\"\n",
        "date: today\n",
        "format:\n",
        "  html:\n",
        "    toc: true\n",
        "    code-fold: false\n",
        "    embed-resources: true\n",
        "execute:\n",
        "  warning: false\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## Setup"
      ],
      "id": "467c124a"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "import pandas as pd\n",
        "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt"
      ],
      "id": "a56bcddf",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Variables and printing"
      ],
      "id": "37b3a5e0"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# variables of different types\n",
        "\n",
        "concentration = 100.0     # a floating-point number\n",
        "day = 0                   # an integer\n",
        "site = \"Waikato River\"    # a string\n",
        "is_tidal = True           # a boolean\n",
        "\n",
        "print(concentration)\n",
        "print(day, site, is_tidal)"
      ],
      "id": "0d43a9e8",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# f-strings for formatted printing\n",
        "\n",
        "day = 5\n",
        "concentration = 47.6183\n",
        "\n",
        "print(f\"Day {day}: concentration = {concentration} mg/L\")       # no rounding\n",
        "print(f\"Day {day}: concentration = {concentration:.2f} mg/L\")   # round to 2 decimals\n",
        "print(f\"Day {day}: concentration = {concentration:.0f} mg/L\")   # round to whole"
      ],
      "id": "f527011e",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Numbers, arithmetic, and rounding"
      ],
      "id": "526f1ae7"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "a = 7\n",
        "b = 2\n",
        "\n",
        "print(a + b, a - b, a * b, a / b, a // b, a ** b)\n",
        "#   sum, diff, product, division, floor-div, power"
      ],
      "id": "df26b73b",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# common rounding functions\n",
        "x = 3.14159\n",
        "\n",
        "print(round(x, 2))        # 3.14   — built-in rounding\n",
        "print(round(x))           # 3      — no argument = to integer\n",
        "print(int(x))             # 3      — truncates towards zero\n",
        "\n",
        "round(3.5) # python uses \"bankers rounding\" rounds to nearest even number\n",
        "round(2.5) "
      ],
      "id": "00a6cb66",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Lists\n",
        "\n",
        "A list holds several values in order. You will use lists to store time series before we switch to NumPy."
      ],
      "id": "4d606229"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "values = [100.0, 95.0, 90.25, 85.7]\n",
        "\n",
        "print(values)\n",
        "print(values[0])       # first item (index 0)\n",
        "print(values[1])       # second item\n",
        "print(values[-1])      # last item\n",
        "print(values[-2])      # second last\n",
        "\n",
        "print(len(values))     # how many items in the list"
      ],
      "id": "5c34ab76",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Slicing — grab a range with [start:stop] (stop is exclusive):"
      ],
      "id": "1c5b3336"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "values = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 70, 80]\n",
        "\n",
        "print(values[0:3])     # [10, 20, 30]  first three\n",
        "print(values[2:5])     # [30, 40, 50]  middle\n",
        "print(values[:4])      # [10, 20, 30, 40]  from the start\n",
        "print(values[4:])      # [50, 60, 70, 80]  to the end\n",
        "print(values[-3:])     # [60, 70, 80]  last three"
      ],
      "id": "1eb9e61e",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Add to a list with `append()`:"
      ],
      "id": "d0fa8bc9"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "history = [100.0]\n",
        "history.append(95.0)\n",
        "history.append(90.25)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(history)\n",
        "print(f\"Started at {history[0]}, ended at {history[-1]}\") # gives the first and last values"
      ],
      "id": "f84dd11c",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 'for' loops\n",
        "\n",
        "A loop repeats an action. This is the engine of every numerical model in this course.\n",
        "\n",
        "The simplest form loops over a range of integers:"
      ],
      "id": "c65cab67"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "for step in range(5):       # goes from 0 to 4 (5 steps)\n",
        "    print(f\"Step {step}\")"
      ],
      "id": "dc60ae78",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "You can also loop over the items in a list — very useful when you have a set of parameter values or site names:"
      ],
      "id": "9eaa7531"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "for k in [0.02, 0.05, 0.10, 0.20]:\n",
        "    print(f\"Decay rate k = {k}\")"
      ],
      "id": "41ee1a02",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "sites = [\"Waikato\", \"Waipa\", \"Piako\"]\n",
        "for name in sites:\n",
        "    print(f\"Loading data for {name}\")"
      ],
      "id": "12b432d3",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "`range(start, stop, step)` also takes a step size — handy for skipping every second value:"
      ],
      "id": "c640e2b5"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "for day in range(0, 20, 5):     # 0, 5, 10, 15\n",
        "    print(f\"Day {day}: take a sample\")"
      ],
      "id": "2705fa7b",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Sum values as you loop. This pattern appears constantly in modelling:"
      ],
      "id": "90b57bed"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "total = 0\n",
        "for value in [3.2, 4.1, 2.8, 5.5]:\n",
        "    total = total + value\n",
        "print(f\"Sum = {total}\")\n",
        "print(f\"Mean = {total / 4}\")"
      ],
      "id": "c28f78e3",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Here is a loop that updates a state variable each step. Read this cell carefully — every model in this part looks like it.\n",
        "\n",
        "The pattern is:\n",
        "\n",
        "A **state variable** (concentration)\n",
        "A **rule** for its rate of change (- decay_rate * concentration)\n",
        "A **loop** over time\n",
        "A **history** list so we can plot it later"
      ],
      "id": "0778dfe9"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "concentration = 100.0\n",
        "decay_rate = 0.05\n",
        "dt = 1.0\n",
        "history = [concentration]     # store the starting value\n",
        "\n",
        "for step in range(10):\n",
        "    concentration = concentration - decay_rate * concentration * dt\n",
        "    history.append(concentration)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(history)"
      ],
      "id": "069f99e0",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Rounding a whole list\n",
        "\n",
        "Printing 4 decimal places for every step is noisy. Two common ways to clean it up.\n",
        "\n",
        "Both do the same thing. Start with option 2 until list comprehensions feel natural, then use option 1."
      ],
      "id": "85b920f6"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# 1. A list comprehension (concise)\n",
        "rounded = [round(c, 2) for c in history]\n",
        "print(rounded)\n",
        "\n",
        "# 2. A plain for loop (verbose but clearer for beginners)\n",
        "rounded = []\n",
        "for c in history:\n",
        "    rounded.append(round(c, 2))\n",
        "\n",
        "print(rounded)"
      ],
      "id": "b3706f29",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Functions\n",
        "\n",
        "A function packages reusable logic under a name. Once you have written it, you can call it as many times as you like without repeating the code.\n",
        "\n",
        "**How to build a function**\n",
        "Every function you write has the same five parts. Read this diagram before the examples:"
      ],
      "id": "8a3158d5"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "def celsius_to_kelvin(t_c):   # 1. def   2. name   3. (inputs):\n",
        "    result = t_c + 273.15     # 4. the body — indented, does the work\n",
        "    return result             # 5. return — hands a value back"
      ],
      "id": "c9116046",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "1. `def` — the keyword that tells Python “I am defining a function”.\n",
        "2. A **name** — you choose it. Use lowercase words joined by underscores (`celsius_to_kelvin`, `decay_step`). The name should say what the function does.\n",
        "3. **Inputs in parentheses, then a colon** — the names inside () are the values the function needs. These are called parameters. End the line with :.\n",
        "4. The **body** — one or more lines, each **indented** (4 spaces). This is the work the function does.\n",
        "5. `return` — hands one value back to whoever called the function. Without return, the function does its work but gives you nothing back.\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{.callout-important}\n",
        "The indentation is not decoration — it is how Python knows which lines belong to the function. Every line of the body must be indented by the same amount. VS Code adds the 4 spaces for you when you press Enter after the :.\n",
        ":::\n"
      ],
      "id": "ba3598ab"
    }
  ],
